/target
/target-tts
**/*.rs.bk
# benchmarking scratch: corpora audio is fetched, never committed (the
# hash-pinned manifest in corpora/*.toml is the committed artifact)
/corpora/clips/
/.venv-bench/
# NOTE: bench/ledger.jsonl is deliberately NOT ignored. "Every claim traces
# to a ledger line" only holds if the ledger ships with the repo.
# model weights are data, not code — they live in the cache, never the repo
*.safetensors
*.gguf
*.onnx
*.bin
# ...but the ORACLE FIXTURES are code's evidence, not weights. `*.bin` above
# exists to keep 77-488 MB checkpoints out; it also silently caught these,
# so three Carmenta oracle tests could only pass on a machine that happened
# to have them and failed on every fresh clone and in CI. For a project that
# gates everything on oracles, an oracle that cannot run is worse than one
# that fails. 1.2 MB total, and the exception is scoped to this one directory
# so a stray checkpoint still cannot ride in.
!/corpora/refs/fixtures/*.bin
/.whispercpp/
/.tools-bench/
/corpora/fonts/
/.venv-diana/
# Detection bench cache: the YOLO26 checkpoints and the COCO annotation zip.
# The checkpoints are AGPL-3.0 and this repo is permissive — Diana never
# vendors or redistributes them (docs/diana-mission-plan.md §7), and `*.bin`
# above does not cover `.pt`, so without this line a `git add -A` would
# commit them. Everything here is fetched by tools/diana_coco_corpus.py and
# tools/diana_export_onnx.py.
/corpora/cache/
# TTS bench artifacts: generated audio is reproducible from the ledger line
# (voice hash + argv + corpus); the piper voice cache is fetched, never committed
/bench/tts-out/
/bench/mt1-substitution/
/.piper-voices/

# Mobile-det intermediates: regenerable by tools/carmenta_mobiledet_oracle.py
# and _ref.py, and large (12 MB of float activations). The fixtures the Rust
# oracle actually reads — the crop, its probability map, the reference boxes,
# and the measured-constant JSONs — ARE tracked.
corpora/refs/fixtures/mobiledet_probmap.npz
corpora/refs/fixtures/mobiledet_stages.npz

# Diana oracle activations: float tensors, regenerable by
# tools/diana_oracle_dump.py from the checkpoint plus a formula. The TRACKED
# artifacts are yolo26*_arch.json (the architecture read off the checkpoint)
# and yolo26*_oracle_digest.json (per-tensor shape/stats + 256 exact values
# at fixed indices) — together they let the Rust oracle run a real gate on a
# fresh clone, which is the standard the Carmenta fixture incident set,
# while the full-tensor max-abs comparison upgrades automatically when the
# npz files are present.
#
# GLOB, not two file names. This was two literal `yolo26n_...` lines while
# the port was n-only; the moment m/l/x were oracled it silently stopped
# covering anything, and `git add -A` would have staged 700 MB of dumps.
# An ignore rule enumerated per instance is a rule that expires the first
# time the thing it protects against gets a second instance.
corpora/refs/fixtures/yolo26*_oracle_*.npz
tools/__pycache__/
